WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.380
- [Kim] Hi, this is Kim from Khan Academy,

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:03.790
and today, we're learning more

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.140
about Brown v. Board
of Education of Topeka.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:12.140
Decided in 1954, Brown v.
Board was a landmark case

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.370
that opened the door for desegregation

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.460
and the modern civil rights movement.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.030
In Brown, the Supreme Court
ruled that segregated schools

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.940
for white and black children,

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.670
which had been prevalent
throughout the American South

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:28.070
since the 1896 decision
in Plessy v. Ferguson

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:32.410
legalized segregation, were
in fact, inherently unequal.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.340
The named plaintiff in
this case, Oliver Brown,

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.780
was the father of Linda,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.580
a third grader who had to take a bus

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.490
to a segregated elementary school

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.390
that was much further from her home

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.560
than the nearby school for white children.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.550
To learn more about the Brown case,

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.550
I sought out the help of two experts.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.460
Theodore Shaw is the Julius L. Chambers

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.270
Distinguished Professor of Law

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.400
and Director of the
Center for Civil Rights

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.140
at the University of North
Carolina School of Law.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.090
Michael McConnell is the
Richard and Frances Mallery

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.600
Professor and Director of
the Constitutional Law Center

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.950
at Stanford Law School,
and a Senior Fellow

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.620
at the Hoover Institution.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.320
So, Professor Shaw, can you just kind of

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.040
set the stage for us?

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.570
What was the overall social and political

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.230
context behind this case?

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.340
- [Theodore] Well, Brown was decided

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.840
in the Cold War Era, I
think that's important

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.150
for reasons that I'll come back to,

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:27.100
but it also was decided,
I guess it was 58 years

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.400
after the Supreme Court ruled

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.580
in Plessy v. Ferguson
that "separate but equal"

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.380
railway cars were constitutional.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:41.240
Of course that extended
to all walks of life

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.240
in the South, and so what you had was

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.590
Jim Crow segregation sustained

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:51.360
and set in place by law.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.341
And Brown was the case
that cracked the edifice

00:01:55.341 --> 00:01:58.470
of Jim Crow segregation,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.570
so it was an enormously important case,

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:05.060
it came after a long campaign

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.960
by the NAACP Legal Defense
Fund to overturn Plessy.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.030
- [Kim] One thing that I think is

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.030
really confusing about Plessy v. Ferguson

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.090
is how it interacts
with the 14th Amendment.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:19.840
So, immediately after the Civil War

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.080
Congress passed and the states ratified

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.107
the 14th Amendment, which gives

00:02:25.107 --> 00:02:29.110
"equal protection under
the law" to all citizens,

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.960
but then Plessy v. Ferguson, just a couple

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.770
decades later, legalizes segregation.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:37.160
Can you talk a little bit more

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.540
about how the Equal Protection Clause

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:44.644
of the 14th Amendment has been interpreted

00:02:44.644 --> 00:02:47.560
in Plessy and then
later in Brown v. Board?

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.290
- [Michael] The Doctrine
of "separate but equal"

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:54.559
was itself a departure from the prior

00:02:54.559 --> 00:02:58.460
understanding of what
the 14th Amendment meant.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.000
In the immediate wake
of the 14th Amendment

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.330
segregation was understood
to be a violation

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.370
of equal rights, thus the
Congress of the United States

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:13.620
passed the Civil Rights Act of 1875,

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.079
which prohibited mandatory segregation

00:03:18.079 --> 00:03:21.890
of, among other things, railroads.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.690
Now, that was struck down in 1883,

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:29.070
I believe it was 1886, but for reasons

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:33.100
not related to "separate
but equal", or segregation.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:37.229
But it's only in the late 1880s and 1890s

00:03:37.229 --> 00:03:41.290
that states begin passing
Jim Crow legislation

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:44.850
like the law in Plessy v. Ferguson.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:48.500
I think Plessy very
likely would have been,

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.100
would have come out a different way

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.470
had it come up 25 years earlier.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.380
It's really very difficult to understand

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.380
the logic of Plessy because Plessy takes

00:04:00.443 --> 00:04:05.443
the point of view that segregation is okay

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.640
because it treats everyone the same way

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.410
and the sense of inferiority or insult

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:17.410
that comes from segregation,
the Court just ignored.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.250
You can read the opinion different ways,

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:25.700
and people do, and have read
the opinion different ways.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.240
A portion of the opinion
makes it sound as though

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.010
this is a kind of social
science conclusion,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:37.010
that segregation breeds
a feeling of inferiority

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.740
that has a deleterious effect

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:46.120
on the black students'
performance in school.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:47.752
- [Theodore] There's a famous footnote

00:04:47.752 --> 00:04:50.660
in Brown v. Board of
Education, footnote 11

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:55.070
in which some social
scientists, and particularly

00:04:56.289 --> 00:05:00.340
a Dr. Kenneth Clark,
did a series of tests,

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.300
a doll test, in which they showed

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.690
black and white dolls who
were otherwise identical

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.790
to little children, little black children,

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:11.757
and they asked "Who was the good doll?

00:05:11.757 --> 00:05:13.930
"Who was the bad doll?
Who was the pretty doll?

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.710
Who was the ugly doll?"
and the black children

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.360
would invariably choose the white dolls

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.450
as the better dolls and the black dolls

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:26.120
as the inferior dolls, and then finally

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.540
the bombshell question,
"Which one looks like you?"

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.460
and they would stop and choose

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:38.460
the black doll, and many times tears

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.890
would come to their eyes,
and so this old notion

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:45.443
of the inferiority that segregation imbued

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.140
in black schoolchildren was important

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:51.460
and the Supreme Court had some

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.860
very eloquent language about that.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.640
- [Michael] Everyone
knew that the only reason

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.690
to have laws requiring black and white

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:04.670
to be separate was because of the feeling

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:07.130
on the part of the dominant white group

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:10.730
that the African race was inferior.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:14.140
I think it was a failure
of political will, really,

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.370
rather than a matter of constitutional

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.100
or legal logic that led to that.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.560
And then it took, what, almost 60 years

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.810
before the Supreme Court
would turn itself around.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.690
- [Theodore] The late A. Leon Higgenbotham

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:32.160
who sat on the Court of
Appeals for the Third Circuit

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.817
in Philadelphia, he used to say that

00:06:34.817 --> 00:06:36.917
"Plessy was wrong the day it was decided."

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:41.180
and I believe he was right about that.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.740
If you go back and look at the rationale

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.570
stated in Plessy, it doesn't hold up,

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.023
it's not an intellectually
honest decision.

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:55.890
And having said that,
if you look at Brown,

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:01.200
it really did not overrule
Plessy on its face

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:07.663
with respect to segregation
and Jim Crow across the board.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:11.010
What it did was say that in the field

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.620
of public education, separate but equal

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.500
is unconstitutional.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.260
Separate schools can not be equal.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:22.730
And what followed Brown
was a series of cases

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:26.020
in all kinds of areas in which the Court,

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:28.250
sometimes without much of an opinion,

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.700
cited Brown but struck down segregation

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:35.040
in public libraries and
public accommodations,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:36.670
and transportation, et cetera.

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:41.170
The case that settled the long-running

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:46.100
boycott in Montgomery, Alabama

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:48.730
that catapulted Martin Luther King

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:52.870
into the national and
world stage, you know,

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:54.970
it was decided, it was resolved finally.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:56.800
The boycott was important,
but it was resolved

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.187
by this case that the
Supreme Court decided

00:08:00.187 --> 00:08:01.760
and it cited Brown.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.960
- [Kim] So Thurgood Marshall
and the Legal Defense Fund

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:07.890
had kind of been chipping
away at the edges

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.120
of segregation, and then they turned

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:13.860
directly to school segregation
in the Brown cases.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.490
So what kind of arguments did they use

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:19.200
to challenge the system
of school segregation?

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.820
- [Michael] Thurgood
Marshall and his co-counsel

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:27.530
in the NAACP established
a very careful strategy

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:30.360
of one case at a time, sort
of pushing the envelope

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:34.810
of this idea that
segregation is inconsistent

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:38.280
with the demands of equal protection.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:43.280
And the interesting
thing about Brown itself,

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:46.500
the case challenging segregation

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:50.900
in Topeka, Kansas, you
know, why Topeka, Kansas?

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:55.790
Why was this such an excellent place

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:57.360
to raise a challenge?

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.360
And the reason is that
the schools in Topeka

00:09:02.906 --> 00:09:07.906
were very substantially equal in quality.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.390
In Topeka they actually had integrated

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:11.930
high schools and middle schools,

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:14.470
it was only the elementary schools

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:19.200
that were segregated, and
the District Court found

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:21.120
and the Supreme Court did not disagree,

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:24.994
that in terms of their material advantages

00:09:24.994 --> 00:09:27.490
the black school was essentially

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:29.620
just as good as the white school.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.640
And that's why the NAACP wanted to

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.040
go after Topeka, because then it

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.110
went to the principle of the thing.

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:40.220
You know, it's one thing
to attack segregation

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:41.900
when "separate but equal" is really

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:44.560
just a fiction, but when
"separate but equal"

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:47.949
is a reality, at least pretty close to it,

00:09:47.949 --> 00:09:50.240
as in Topeka, you have to go after

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.730
the actual principle that segregation

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:56.261
is wrong in principle and
not just wrong because

00:09:56.261 --> 00:09:58.680
it tends most of the time to lead to

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.940
material disadvantage.

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:03.620
- [Theodore] Brown was
actually five cases.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:06.670
The Brown case itself
came from Topeka, Kansas.

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.930
In many ways the Supreme Court might have

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.530
chosen to lead with the Brown case

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:13.570
because it wasn't part of the South,

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:16.040
and so in some ways it
might have been viewed as

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.680
taking pressure off of the South.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:21.962
But there were five cases altogether.

00:10:21.962 --> 00:10:26.130
There was a case out of South Carolina,

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:29.810
a case out of Virginia,
a case out of Delaware,

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:31.490
a case out of Washington, D.C,

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:34.070
and then the Topeka case.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:37.000
And the Brown family was actually not even

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.410
the only family named
Brown in this Topeka case.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:46.000
And the other Brown case did not have

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.000
their father available as the lead voice

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:54.560
and they wanted a man, and so

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:57.110
Oliver Brown was the one they chose.

00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:58.790
They stipulated that the schools

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:01.630
were, in fact, equal, in all respects.

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:04.740
And of course they weren't,
it was never possible

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:06.820
to make "separate but equal" equal.

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:09.370
But they stipulated that these schools

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:11.500
were equal, the black
and the white schools,

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:14.688
because they wanted the
court to be able to,

00:11:14.688 --> 00:11:18.200
or to have to, confront the issue

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:20.700
of whether "separate but equal" per se

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:22.520
was unconstitutional.

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:25.260
- [Kim] Interesting, so
it's this place where

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:28.060
they're hoping not to have a ruling that,

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:31.320
for example, oh the
schools for black children

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:33.840
need to be brought up
to code to be the same

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:35.230
as white children, but they're trying

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:39.470
to strike down the idea
of segregation altogether.

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:42.770
And indeed, they rule unanimously that

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:45.180
separate but equal facilities are

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:47.340
inherently unequal.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:50.047
- [Michael] The Supreme
Court in Brown says

00:11:50.047 --> 00:11:54.940
"Well, whatever may
have been the status of

00:11:55.867 --> 00:11:59.347
"education as a civil right back then,

00:11:59.347 --> 00:12:02.367
"back in the 19th century, by now

00:12:02.367 --> 00:12:04.607
"it surely is a civil right and therefore

00:12:04.607 --> 00:12:09.607
"there can be no discrimination
with regard to it."

00:12:10.202 --> 00:12:11.910
And one other thing that changed,

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:14.440
that I think the Supreme
Court doesn't refer to

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:17.310
but many historians think was surely

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.590
in the back of their minds has to do with

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:21.540
foreign policy, of all things,

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:24.640
that during this time the United States

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.850
in the wake of World War II was playing

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:31.840
a very major role in opposing colonialism,

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:36.110
opposing communism, and trying to spread

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:39.650
the gospel of liberty around the world,

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:44.650
and when we had segregation at home

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:47.210
this looked like rank hypocrisy.

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:49.920
- [Theodore] This was the Cold War period.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:52.950
These soldiers came home from fighting

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:57.950
a form of racism in Europe, Nazi Germany,

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:02.450
you know, the Holocaust,
and when they came back

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:04.593
they came back to segregated America.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:08.590
And there are many
stories that are painful

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:11.290
about what their experiences were.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:13.440
But meanwhile the United States is having

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:16.590
this Cold War with the Soviet Union,

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.710
and the Soviet Union was exploiting

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:22.250
this inconsistency in what America

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.480
said it was and what it was.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:25.830
And although the Supreme Court

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:28.853
never said a word about
that in the Brown cases,

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:33.900
that was very much
context for that decision.

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:37.010
- [Kim] So what did it
take for desegregation

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:39.160
to really come about?

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.030
Do you think we have
achieved desegregation?

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:43.230
- [Theodore] Yes and no.

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:48.740
There was a long battle
to implement Brown,

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:52.890
and in fact for the
most part Brown was not

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:56.210
implemented fully until,
to the extent it ever was,

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:59.460
until the early 1970s,
when the Supreme Court

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:00.540
decided a case called

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:04.040
Swann v. Charlotte-Mecklenburg
Board of Education

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:07.810
in North Carolina, and
sanctioned the use of busing.

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:09.860
- [Michael] This does not depend upon

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:13.080
what social scientists might think about

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:14.480
how people do in school.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:16.210
This is a much more fundamental

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:19.350
principle of equality under the law,

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:24.350
that our law must treat
black and white the same.

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:28.680
That for the government to
draw racial distinctions

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:33.480
is fundamentally unequal
and unconstitutional

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.243
and wrong and unjust.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:39.610
Fundamentally so, and
not just as a matter of

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:44.170
social science evidence.

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:47.400
- [Theodore] I don't
want to sound despairing,

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:51.681
you know, but I think if anybody looks at

00:14:51.681 --> 00:14:56.681
our public schools today
in many of our communities,

00:14:56.980 --> 00:14:59.980
in our towns and cities, and our counties,

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.110
we have to acknowledge, honestly,

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:05.280
that there's still a
great deal of segregation,

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:09.330
and there are many schools that are

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:12.360
identifiably white or identifiably black

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:17.360
and now, you know Latinos,
Hispanics, a large degree

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:20.420
of segregation there also.

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:24.270
And even voluntary desegregation efforts

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:27.830
have been found by the
Supreme Court a few years ago

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:31.020
to be largely unconstitutional and illegal

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:32.043
and that's tragic.

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:36.340
But that doesn't mean that
Brown wasn't significant.

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:39.800
I was born in the year of
Brown v. Board of Education.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:42.750
The America that I grew
up in is a very different

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:46.335
country than the one that preceded it.

00:15:46.335 --> 00:15:49.660
In spite of our flaws and our warts,

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:51.870
no Brown v. Board of Education,

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:55.600
no civil rights movement, I would say.

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:58.870
And ultimately no desegregation of

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:01.440
our society to the extent
that we've accomplished it.

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:05.240
And we have, to a great
degree, no Barack Obama.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:09.180
So I think Brown stands, as I said, as a

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:13.760
dividing point in America,
but we can do better.

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.650
- [Kim] So we've learned
that under the leadership

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:18.080
of Thurgood Marshall and the NAACP

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:20.200
Legal Defense Fund, the decision

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:22.550
in Brown v. Board of Education struck

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.160
a major blow to the system of segregation.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:27.970
After World War II, amidst the ideological

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:30.530
struggle of the Cold
War, the Supreme Court

00:16:30.530 --> 00:16:33.870
overturned the precedent
set in Plessy v. Ferguson

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:36.620
and ruled that segregated
education constituted

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:39.330
a violation of the Equal Protection Clause

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:40.810
of the 14th Amendment.

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:43.250
To learn more about Brown v. Board,

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:45.300
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:47.730
interactive Constitution
and Khan Academy's

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:50.353
resources on U.S. government and politics.

